WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.722
The lamp could warm the water, so
I'd accidentally test two factors.

00:00:01.576 --> 00:00:02.360
Exactly.

00:00:02.679 --> 00:00:05.887
Monitor temperature, allow
the plant to settle, repeat

00:00:05.887 --> 00:00:07.698
readings, and calculate a mean.

00:00:08.256 --> 00:00:10.975
Follow your teacher's setup
and keep electrical equipment

00:00:10.977 --> 00:00:12.387
safely away from water.

00:00:12.825 --> 00:00:17.836
A graph needs labeled axes, units where
relevant, and an explanation of the shape.

00:00:18.575 --> 00:00:21.808
No motivation poster
required, just a fair test.

00:00:22.164 --> 00:00:24.480
We cannot see a leaf making glucose.

00:00:24.874 --> 00:00:28.786
How can we investigate whether
photosynthesis has produced stored food?

00:00:29.642 --> 00:00:31.444
Test for starch with iodine.

00:00:31.894 --> 00:00:34.017
Blue-black means starch is present.

00:00:34.692 --> 00:00:35.236
Yes.

00:00:35.506 --> 00:00:38.261
Without starch, iodine stays orange-brown.

00:00:38.642 --> 00:00:42.055
Before comparing treatments, keep
the plant in darkness long enough

00:00:42.057 --> 00:00:43.607
to use up its starch stores.

00:00:43.838 --> 00:00:45.353
We call that de-starching.

00:00:46.098 --> 00:00:49.688
Otherwise, an old stash of starch
could make a covered leaf look as if

00:00:49.688 --> 00:00:51.909
it had photosynthesized in darkness.

00:00:52.705 --> 00:00:53.543
Exactly.

00:00:53.905 --> 00:00:57.400
This is a supervised school
laboratory demonstration, not

00:00:57.400 --> 00:00:58.853
a solo kitchen experiment.

00:00:59.537 --> 00:01:02.102
First, the teacher puts the
leaf in hot water to kill it

00:01:02.337 --> 00:01:03.933
and make its tissues permeable.

00:01:04.274 --> 00:01:08.219
Ethanol removes the green chlorophyll
so we can see the color change.

00:01:09.042 --> 00:01:10.381
Ethanol near a flame?

00:01:10.927 --> 00:01:13.341
That sounds like a terrible
science plot twist.

00:01:14.091 --> 00:01:15.151
It is flammable.

00:01:15.678 --> 00:01:17.779
Never heat it over a naked flame.

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:21.787
A teacher uses the approved hot
water bath procedure with heat

00:01:21.787 --> 00:01:25.745
sources controlled, eye protection,
and laboratory safety rules.

00:01:26.177 --> 00:01:30.242
Then warm water softens the brittle
leaf before iodine is added.

00:01:31.055 --> 00:01:35.079
Water, remove the color
safely, soften, iodine.

00:01:35.618 --> 00:01:38.906
Blue-black means starch,
not glucose directly.

00:01:39.669 --> 00:01:40.569
Good distinction.

00:01:40.907 --> 00:01:44.602
Cover part of a de-starched leaf with
opaque foil and leave it in light.

00:01:44.957 --> 00:01:47.224
Compare covered and uncovered parts.

00:01:47.566 --> 00:01:51.919
If only the exposed part forms starch,
that supports light being necessary.

00:01:52.672 --> 00:01:55.818
A variegated leaf has green
and non-green patches.

00:01:56.270 --> 00:02:00.695
If only green patches form starch,
that supports chlorophyll being needed.

00:02:01.337 --> 00:02:01.769
Right.

00:02:02.129 --> 00:02:05.056
Record the green areas
before removing the pigment.

00:02:05.465 --> 00:02:09.599
To investigate carbon dioxide, a
teacher can compare a leaf in carbon

00:02:09.599 --> 00:02:13.978
dioxide-free conditions with a matched
control that has carbon dioxide.

00:02:14.531 --> 00:02:17.224
Chemical absorbers require
supervised handling.

00:02:17.989 --> 00:02:22.063
Keep the other conditions comparable and
don't let the chemical touch the leaf.

00:02:22.754 --> 00:02:23.309
Correct.

00:02:23.619 --> 00:02:27.809
The conclusion comes from the
comparison, not just one dark patch.

00:02:28.247 --> 00:02:32.983
A positive starch test alone does
not tell us precisely when or where

00:02:33.034 --> 00:02:34.810
every sugar molecule was made.

00:02:35.228 --> 00:02:38.105
Controlled conditions make
our explanations stronger.

00:02:38.573 --> 00:02:39.537
Final challenge.

00:02:39.860 --> 00:02:42.229
At night, does a plant stop respiring?

00:02:43.016 --> 00:02:43.308
No.

00:02:43.587 --> 00:02:45.614
Plants respire day and night.

00:02:46.123 --> 00:02:49.265
Photosynthesis needs light,
so it stops in darkness.

00:02:50.028 --> 00:02:50.874
Exactly.

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:54.909
In bright light, photosynthesis
can be faster than respiration.

00:02:55.401 --> 00:02:59.068
Overall, the plant takes in carbon
dioxide and releases oxygen.

00:02:59.529 --> 00:03:03.818
In darkness, respiration uses
oxygen and releases carbon dioxide.

00:03:04.603 --> 00:03:06.811
At very low light, they might balance.

00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:11.250
No net gas exchange, but both
processes can still be happening.

00:03:12.053 --> 00:03:14.363
That balance is called
the compensation point.

00:03:15.131 --> 00:03:18.305
Zero net exchange does not mean
the plant has switched off.

00:03:18.683 --> 00:03:22.881
The overall equations look like opposites,
but the chemical pathways are different.

00:03:23.604 --> 00:03:28.079
One stores light energy in chemical form,
the other releases energy from food.

00:03:28.413 --> 00:03:30.201
Neither creates energy from nothing.

00:03:30.432 --> 00:03:31.583
Your tissues are safe.

00:03:32.391 --> 00:03:33.701
Let's test the whole lesson.

00:03:34.134 --> 00:03:36.321
Two raw materials, two products.

00:03:36.567 --> 00:03:36.915
Go.

00:03:37.681 --> 00:03:41.635
Carbon dioxide and water
produce glucose and oxygen using

00:03:41.638 --> 00:03:43.335
light absorbed by chlorophyll.

00:03:44.073 --> 00:03:48.202
Where is chlorophyll, and how
does carbon dioxide enter a leaf?

00:03:48.973 --> 00:03:50.991
Chlorophyll is in chloroplasts.

00:03:51.504 --> 00:03:55.182
Carbon dioxide diffuses through
stomata and the air spaces

00:03:55.182 --> 00:03:57.101
toward photosynthesizing cells.

00:03:57.894 --> 00:04:00.405
Why can more light fail
to increase the rate?

00:04:01.153 --> 00:04:05.716
Another factor, such as carbon dioxide
supply or temperature, may be limiting.

00:04:06.173 --> 00:04:09.223
Look at the conditions and
test one change at a time.

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:13.548
What does iodine detect,
and why destarch first?

00:04:14.342 --> 00:04:15.043
Starch.

00:04:15.388 --> 00:04:19.481
Destarching reduces old starch, so a
controlled experiment can relate new

00:04:19.481 --> 00:04:23.835
starch to the treatment, and the ethanol
step belongs in a supervised laboratory.

00:04:24.602 --> 00:04:25.208
Last one.

00:04:25.674 --> 00:04:28.479
Where does much of a growing
tree's carbon come from?

00:04:29.290 --> 00:04:34.446
Carbon dioxide in the air, fixed into
glucose and built into its tissues, not

00:04:34.446 --> 00:04:36.658
from eating a tree-sized mountain of soil.

00:04:37.456 --> 00:04:38.090
Perfect.

00:04:38.539 --> 00:04:40.000
Photosynthesis connects sunlight,
food chains, oxygen, and plant growth.
